1) List

A list stores elements in the order they are inserted.

> List maintains insertion order
> Allows duplicate elements
> Allows null values
> Allows accessing elements using index

    a) ArrayList: Uses a dynamic array internally
                  Fast for retrieving elements
                  Slower for insertion and deletion in the middle
                  Uses less memory compared to Linked List

                  Use ArrayList when:
                  We frequently read data.
                  Insertions/deletions are less frequent.

                  Examples:
                  E-Commerce Shopping Carts - Holds items as a user clicks "Add to Cart".
                  Expands in size when more products are added and shrinks if an item is deleted

                  Music and Video Playlists - Manages streaming queues on apps like Spotify or Netflix.
                  Allows songs or movies to be inserted, deleted, or reordered dynamically.

                  Task & To Do Lists
                  Keeps track of daily chores or work items where tasks are constantly checked off (removed) or added

    b) LinkedList : Uses Doubly Linked List internally
                    Faster for insertion/deletion operations
                    Slower for read operations or random access
                    Uses more memory as it has to store both references (previous & next element)

                    Use Linked List when frequent insertion or deletion operations are required

                    Example: In the real world, linked lists are used when you need to dynamically
                    add or remove items without resizing a fixed container. Java provides a built-in
                    implementation via the java.util.LinkedList class, which operates as a doubly linked list.

                    Music Player PlayList
                    GPS Navigation Routes
                    Undo/Redo Layers

    c) Stack: Follows LIFO - Last In First Out

              The last element to be added in the stack is the first one ro be removed from the stack
              Main operations - push(), pop(), peak()

              push() - to add an element in to the stack
              pop()  - to remove an element from the stack
              peak() - returns the next element that will be removed from the stack

    d) Vector: Legacy data structure

2) Set

Stores unique elements
Does not maintain insertion order
No indexing
No duplicates

Use when we want to store unique data like email, password

    a) HashSet: Stores unique elements
                Order is not maintained
                Allows only one null value
                Faster for searching

                Use HashSet when uniqueness is important

                Example: E-Commerce (Unique Items in Cart Promo): Applying a "buy 3 unique items" discount code
                         by filtering a shopping cart list for unique Product IDs

    b) LinkedHashSet: Maintains insertion order

    c) TreeSet: Stores elements in sorted order
                Does not allow null values
                - if we add null, it will not throw any error at compile time, but will throw NPE at runtime

                Example: School Management (Student Merit Lists): Generating a real-time leaderboard or honor
                         roll from student IDs sorted automatically by grade rank.

3) Queue: Follows FIFO - First In First Out

    The first element to be added into queue will be the first one to be removed from the queue
    Elements are added at rear end (tail) and are removed from the front end (head)

    Common implementation:
    Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();

    Example: Train ticket queue

    a) Priority Queue: Each element will be associated with a priority,
                       highest priority element will be served first

                       Example: Prime Minister in middle of ticket queue
    b) LinkedList

        Example: A physical line at a theater counter is a perfect example. The customer who arrives
                 first gets the ticket first. New arrivals must join the end of the line.

    c) Deque - ArrayDeque: Internally uses dynamic array to store data

4) Map: Map stores data in key-value pair

        Each key is unique. Values can be duplicated

        a) HashMap - Stores key-value pairs
                     Keys are unique
                     Values can be duplicate
                     Allows one null key
                     Allows multiple null values
                     No ordering

        b) LinkedHashMap - Maintains Insertion order

        c) TreeMap - Stores keys in sorted order
                     Null keys are not allowed

        d) HashTable - No Null Keys or values are allowed
                       Thread-safe





